import Mathlib
/-! QED-E207: Strong shift equivalence of the Baker matrices for every k
For each k >= 1, the 2x2 matrices A_k = [[1, k+1],[k, 1]] and B_k = [[1, k(k+1)],[1, 1]] are similar over the integers and primitive, so their shifts of finite type are shift equivalent. Are the two shifts topologically conjugate for every k, i.e. can A_k be turned into B_k by finitely many moves RS -> SR with nonnegative integer matrices R, S?
Informal statement: Let $A_k=\begin{pmatrix}1&k+1\\k&1\end{pmatrix}$, $B_k=\begin{pmatrix}1&k(k+1)\\1&1\end{pmatrix}$, and let $\sim_{SSE}$ be the equivalence relation on square nonnegative integer matrices of all sizes generated by $RS\approx SR$ ($R,S$ rectangular, nonnegative integer). Determine whether $A_k\sim_{SSE}B_k$ for every integer $k\ge1$.
Formalization notes: A square nonnegative integer matrix of any size is a dependent pair
`⟨m, M⟩ : Σ m : ℕ, Matrix (Fin m) (Fin m) ℕ` (entries in ℕ = nonnegative integers).
Elementary strong shift equivalence (Williams): `elemSSE ⟨m, A⟩ ⟨l, B⟩` iff there are
R : m × l and S : l × m nonnegative integer matrices with A = R S and B = S R. This relation is
symmetric (swap R and S), so its reflexive–transitive closure `Relation.ReflTransGen` is exactly
the equivalence relation it generates, i.e. strong shift equivalence over ℤ₊. By Williams'
theorem, SSE of A and B is equivalent to topological conjugacy of the edge shifts X_A, X_B, so
this is the standard formulation. Intermediate matrices of any size (including 0) are allowed,
as in the standard definition; this cannot matter here since A_k, B_k are primitive.
Claim is the "yes" answer: A_k ~SSE B_k for every k ≥ 1. ¬Claim means some k ≥ 1 with A_k, B_k
not strong shift equivalent (a new conjugacy invariant beyond shift equivalence would be needed).
-/
namespace OpenProblems.QEDE207
noncomputable section

/-- Square matrices over ℕ of arbitrary size. -/
abbrev SqMat : Type := Σ m : ℕ, Matrix (Fin m) (Fin m) ℕ

/-- Elementary strong shift equivalence: A = R S and B = S R with R, S nonnegative integer. -/
def elemSSE (A B : SqMat) : Prop :=
  ∃ (R : Matrix (Fin A.1) (Fin B.1) ℕ) (S : Matrix (Fin B.1) (Fin A.1) ℕ),
    A.2 = R * S ∧ B.2 = S * R

/-- Strong shift equivalence over ℤ₊: the equivalence relation generated by `elemSSE`
(which is symmetric, so the reflexive–transitive closure suffices). -/
def SSE (A B : SqMat) : Prop :=
  Relation.ReflTransGen elemSSE A B

/-- Baker's matrix A_k = [[1, k+1], [k, 1]]. -/
def bakerA (k : ℕ) : SqMat := ⟨2, !![1, k + 1; k, 1]⟩

/-- Baker's matrix B_k = [[1, k(k+1)], [1, 1]]. -/
def bakerB (k : ℕ) : SqMat := ⟨2, !![1, k * (k + 1); 1, 1]⟩

def Claim : Prop :=
  ∀ k : ℕ, 1 ≤ k → SSE (bakerA k) (bakerB k)

end
end OpenProblems.QEDE207
